Chapter: GopQr
  cites Works chapter GopDraw
  cites Works chapter GopAhci
  cites Foreword chapter CCE

 QR encoding for hardware telemetry -- safety recommendation R-1 of
 docs/PM/Done/Stories/TheSilentKeyboard.md. A machine with no serial port
 and no working disk write can still be READ: it renders its findings
 as QR codes on the framebuffer, a human photographs the glass with
 whatever camera is in their pocket, and the decoder turns the photo
 back into exact bytes. No transcription, no OCR, no dependence on
 the subsystem under test. The human-readable status lines stay on
 screen beside the codes, so the person holding the camera also knows
 whether the news is good.

 One fixed shape, no tables: version 5, byte mode, error level L --
 a 37x37 module matrix carrying up to 106 bytes in a single
 Reed-Solomon block of (134, 108). Fixed version means fixed
 alignment pattern, fixed format layout, no version-info block
 (that starts at version 7), and a single code path that a battery
 test can pin. Payloads beyond 106 bytes split across several codes,
 each chunk prefixed "i/n;" so the decoder can reassemble from one
 photograph. Mask pattern 0 is applied unconditionally: mask CHOICE
 is a readability optimization in the spec, not a correctness
 requirement, and a fixed mask keeps the encoder deterministic.

 Everything through the matrix is pure computation over small
 buffers, so the battery can verify it without a device; only the
 final draw touches the framebuffer, through GopDraw.

 We say:

Section: Shape

  qr-size : Integer = 37
  qr-data-cw : Integer = 108
  qr-ecc-cw : Integer = 26
  qr-total-cw : Integer = 134
  qr-cap : Integer = 106

Section: The Field

 GF(256) with the QR polynomial 285. One buffer holds both tables:
 exp at offset 0 (exp of 0..254; index 255 wraps to 1 harmlessly),
 log at offset 256.

  qr-gf-new : Integer
  qr-gf-new =
    let g = alloc-zeroed 640 64
    in qr-gf-fill g 0 1

  qr-gf-fill : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-gf-fill (g) (i) (x) =
    if i >= 256 then g
    else let e = poke-byte g i x
    in let l = if i < 255 then poke-byte g (256 + x) i else 0
    in let x2 = bit-shl x 1
    in let x3 = if x2 >= 256 then bit-and (bit-xor x2 285) 255 else x2
    in qr-gf-fill g (i + 1) x3

  qr-gf-mul : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-gf-mul (g) (a) (b) =
    if a == 0 then 0
    else if b == 0 then 0
    else peek-byte g (int-mod ((peek-byte g (256 + a)) + (peek-byte g (256 + b))) 255)

Section: Reed-Solomon

 The generator polynomial is the product of (x - a^i) for i in
 0..25, built by repeated multiplication so no coefficient table is
 trusted to memory. g[j] is the coefficient of x^j; g[26] ends up 1.

  qr-rs-gen : Integer -> Integer
  qr-rs-gen (gf) =
    let g = alloc-zeroed 32 64
    in let s = poke-byte g 0 1
    in qr-rs-gen-loop gf g 0

  qr-rs-gen-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-rs-gen-loop (gf) (g) (i) =
    if i >= qr-ecc-cw then g
    else let a = peek-byte gf i
    in let u = qr-rs-gen-mul gf g a (i + 1)
    in qr-rs-gen-loop gf g (i + 1)

  qr-rs-gen-mul : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-rs-gen-mul (gf) (g) (a) (j) =
    if j < 0 then 0
    else let cur = peek-byte g j
    in let prev = if j == 0 then 0 else peek-byte g (j - 1)
    in let w = poke-byte g j (bit-xor prev (qr-gf-mul gf cur a))
    in qr-rs-gen-mul gf g a (j - 1)

 Synthetic division of the message (as the high-order coefficients)
 by the generator; what remains in the tail is the 26 ECC codewords.
 The division CONSUMES the message positions as it eliminates each
 leading coefficient (the monic generator's top term XORs work[i]
 with itself), so the data is copied back in afterwards -- the work
 buffer is then the final codeword sequence: data, then ECC.

  qr-rs-encode : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-rs-encode (gf) (gen) (data) =
    let work = alloc-zeroed 144 64
    in let c = qr-copy-bytes data work 0 qr-data-cw
    in let d = qr-rs-div gf gen work 0
    in let r = qr-copy-bytes data work 0 qr-data-cw
    in work

  qr-copy-bytes : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-copy-bytes (src) (dst) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then dst
    else let w = poke-byte dst i (peek-byte src i)
    in qr-copy-bytes src dst (i + 1) n

  qr-rs-div : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-rs-div (gf) (gen) (work) (i) =
    if i >= qr-data-cw then work
    else let f = peek-byte work i
    in let s = if f == 0 then 0 else qr-rs-step gf gen work i f 0
    in qr-rs-div gf gen work (i + 1)

  qr-rs-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-rs-step (gf) (gen) (work) (i) (f) (k) =
    if k > qr-ecc-cw then 0
    else let old = peek-byte work (i + k)
    in let w = poke-byte work (i + k) (bit-xor old (qr-gf-mul gf f (peek-byte gen (qr-ecc-cw - k))))
    in qr-rs-step gf gen work i f (k + 1)

Section: Bits

  qr-put-bits : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-put-bits (buf) (pos) (v) (n) =
    if n <= 0 then pos
    else let bit = bit-and (bit-shru v (n - 1)) 1
    in let byi = bit-shru pos 3
    in let w = if bit == 1 then poke-byte buf byi (bit-or (peek-byte buf byi) (bit-shl 1 (7 - (bit-and pos 7)))) else 0
    in qr-put-bits buf (pos + 1) v (n - 1)

  qr-put-slice-bits : Integer, Integer, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-put-slice-bits (buf) (pos) (s) (i) (stop) =
    if i >= stop then pos
    else let p2 = qr-put-bits buf pos (to-unicode (char-code (char-at s i))) 8
    in qr-put-slice-bits buf p2 s (i + 1) stop

 Byte mode: indicator 0100, an 8-bit count (versions 1-9), the
 bytes, a 4-bit terminator, zero-pad to the byte boundary (already
 zero in a fresh buffer), then the spec's alternating pad bytes
 236 and 17 out to the 108 data codewords. The slice form encodes a
 prefix (a chunk tag like "2/4;") followed by a character range of a
 larger text, so a long payload can spread across several codes
 without a substring ever being built.

  qr-make-data2 : Text, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-make-data2 (prefix) (s) (from) (len) =
    let buf = alloc-zeroed 144 64
    in let n = text-length prefix + len
    in let p1 = qr-put-bits buf 0 4 4
    in let p2 = qr-put-bits buf p1 n 8
    in let p3 = qr-put-slice-bits buf p2 prefix 0 (text-length prefix)
    in let p4 = qr-put-slice-bits buf p3 s from (from + len)
    in let p5 = qr-put-bits buf p4 0 4
    in qr-pad-bytes buf ((p5 + 7) / 8) 0

  qr-make-data : Text -> Integer
  qr-make-data (s) = qr-make-data2 "" s 0 (text-length s)

  qr-pad-bytes : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-pad-bytes (buf) (i) (alt) =
    if i >= qr-data-cw then buf
    else let w = poke-byte buf i (if alt == 0 then 236 else 17)
    in qr-pad-bytes buf (i + 1) (1 - alt)

Section: Format Information

 Fifteen bits: two bits of error level (L = 01), three of mask id,
 ten of BCH remainder (generator 1335), the whole thing XORed with
 the spec constant 21522 so it is never all-zero.

  qr-bch : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-bch (r) (i) =
    if i < 10 then r
    else if bit-and (bit-shru r i) 1 == 1 then qr-bch (bit-xor r (bit-shl 1335 (i - 10))) (i - 1)
    else qr-bch r (i - 1)

  qr-format-bits : Integer -> Integer
  qr-format-bits (mask) =
    let d = bit-or 8 mask
    in let r = qr-bch (bit-shl d 10) 14
    in bit-xor (bit-or (bit-shl d 10) r) 21522

Section: Matrix

 Two parallel 37x37 byte planes: m holds the modules (1 dark), fn
 marks function modules the data placement must step around. The
 reserved rectangles overlap the timing lines at two corners; the
 flag is idempotent so the overlap is harmless.

  qr-at : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-at (r) (c) = r * qr-size + c

  qr-mark-rect : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-mark-rect (fn) (r0) (c0) (h) (w) (i) =
    if i >= h * w then 0
    else let m = poke-byte fn (qr-at (r0 + i / w) (c0 + int-mod i w)) 1
    in qr-mark-rect fn r0 c0 h w (i + 1)

  qr-finder : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-finder (m) (r0) (c0) (i) =
    if i >= 49 then 0
    else let r = i / 7
    in let c = int-mod i 7
    in let dark = if r == 0 then 1 else if r == 6 then 1 else if c == 0 then 1 else if c == 6 then 1
      else if r >= 2 then (if r <= 4 then (if c >= 2 then (if c <= 4 then 1 else 0) else 0) else 0) else 0
    in let w = poke-byte m (qr-at (r0 + r) (c0 + c)) dark
    in qr-finder m r0 c0 (i + 1)

  qr-align : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-align (m) (i) =
    if i >= 25 then 0
    else let r = i / 5
    in let c = int-mod i 5
    in let dark = if r == 0 then 1 else if r == 4 then 1 else if c == 0 then 1 else if c == 4 then 1
      else if r == 2 then (if c == 2 then 1 else 0) else 0
    in let w = poke-byte m (qr-at (28 + r) (28 + c)) dark
    in qr-align m (i + 1)

  qr-timing : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-timing (m) (fn) (i) =
    if i > 28 then 0
    else let dark = if int-mod i 2 == 0 then 1 else 0
    in let a = poke-byte m (qr-at 6 i) dark
    in let b = poke-byte m (qr-at i 6) dark
    in let fa = poke-byte fn (qr-at 6 i) 1
    in let fb = poke-byte fn (qr-at i 6) 1
    in qr-timing m fn (i + 1)

  qr-functions : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-functions (m) (fn) =
    let a = qr-mark-rect fn 0 0 9 9 0
    in let b = qr-mark-rect fn 0 29 9 8 0
    in let c = qr-mark-rect fn 29 0 8 9 0
    in let d = qr-mark-rect fn 28 28 5 5 0
    in let t = qr-timing m fn 8
    in let f1 = qr-finder m 0 0 0
    in let f2 = qr-finder m 0 30 0
    in let f3 = qr-finder m 30 0 0
    in let al = qr-align m 0
    in poke-byte m (qr-at 29 8) 1

Section: Data Placement

 The standard zigzag: two-module columns from the right edge, up
 then down, hopping over the timing column between the pairs (8,7)
 and (5,4). Data bits stream MSB-first out of the 134 codewords;
 the seven remainder bits past the end are zero. Mask 0 flips a
 module when row + column is even, applied only to data modules --
 which is every module this walk touches.

  qr-data-bit : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-data-bit (cw) (idx) =
    if idx >= qr-total-cw * 8 then 0
    else bit-and (bit-shru (peek-byte cw (idx / 8)) (7 - (bit-and idx 7))) 1

  qr-place : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-place (m) (fn) (cw) = qr-place-cols m fn cw 36 1 0

  qr-place-cols : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-place-cols (m) (fn) (cw) (col) (up) (bit) =
    if col < 1 then bit
    else let b2 = qr-place-col m fn cw col up 0 bit
    in let next = if col == 8 then 5 else col - 2
    in qr-place-cols m fn cw next (1 - up) b2

  qr-place-col : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-place-col (m) (fn) (cw) (col) (up) (i) (bit) =
    if i >= qr-size then bit
    else let row = if up == 1 then qr-size - 1 - i else i
    in let b2 = qr-place-one m fn cw row col bit
    in let b3 = qr-place-one m fn cw row (col - 1) b2
    in qr-place-col m fn cw col up (i + 1) b3

  qr-place-one : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-place-one (m) (fn) (cw) (row) (col) (bit) =
    if peek-byte fn (qr-at row col) /= 0 then bit
    else let v = qr-data-bit cw bit
    in let masked = if int-mod (row + col) 2 == 0 then bit-xor v 1 else v
    in let w = poke-byte m (qr-at row col) masked
    in bit + 1

Section: Format Placement

 Both copies, most significant bit first, exactly where the spec
 puts them. The second copy runs up the left edge of the bottom-left
 finder and across the bottom of the top-right one.

  qr-fmt-bit : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-fmt-bit (fmt) (k) = bit-and (bit-shru fmt k) 1

  qr-place-format : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-place-format (m) (fmt) =
    let a0 = poke-byte m (qr-at 8 0) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 14)
    in let a1 = poke-byte m (qr-at 8 1) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 13)
    in let a2 = poke-byte m (qr-at 8 2) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 12)
    in let a3 = poke-byte m (qr-at 8 3) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 11)
    in let a4 = poke-byte m (qr-at 8 4) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 10)
    in let a5 = poke-byte m (qr-at 8 5) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 9)
    in let a6 = poke-byte m (qr-at 8 7) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 8)
    in let a7 = poke-byte m (qr-at 8 8) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 7)
    in let a8 = poke-byte m (qr-at 7 8) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 6)
    in let a9 = poke-byte m (qr-at 5 8) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 5)
    in let aa = poke-byte m (qr-at 4 8) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 4)
    in let ab = poke-byte m (qr-at 3 8) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 3)
    in let ac = poke-byte m (qr-at 2 8) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 2)
    in let ad = poke-byte m (qr-at 1 8) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 1)
    in let ae = poke-byte m (qr-at 0 8) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 0)
    in let b0 = poke-byte m (qr-at 36 8) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 14)
    in let b1 = poke-byte m (qr-at 35 8) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 13)
    in let b2 = poke-byte m (qr-at 34 8) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 12)
    in let b3 = poke-byte m (qr-at 33 8) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 11)
    in let b4 = poke-byte m (qr-at 32 8) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 10)
    in let b5 = poke-byte m (qr-at 31 8) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 9)
    in let b6 = poke-byte m (qr-at 30 8) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 8)
    in let b7 = poke-byte m (qr-at 8 29) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 7)
    in let b8 = poke-byte m (qr-at 8 30) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 6)
    in let b9 = poke-byte m (qr-at 8 31) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 5)
    in let ba = poke-byte m (qr-at 8 32) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 4)
    in let bb = poke-byte m (qr-at 8 33) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 3)
    in let bc = poke-byte m (qr-at 8 34) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 2)
    in let bd = poke-byte m (qr-at 8 35) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 1)
    in poke-byte m (qr-at 8 36) (qr-fmt-bit fmt 0)

Section: Encode

 Text in, 37x37 module plane out (byte per module, 1 dark). The
 caller may keep the buffer as long as it likes; nothing here is
 retained.

  qr5-encode-part : Text, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr5-encode-part (prefix) (s) (from) (len) =
    let gf = qr-gf-new
    in let gen = qr-rs-gen gf
    in let data = qr-make-data2 prefix s from len
    in let cw = qr-rs-encode gf gen data
    in let m = alloc-zeroed 1400 64
    in let fn = alloc-zeroed 1400 64
    in let f = qr-functions m fn
    in let p = qr-place m fn cw
    in let fmt = qr-place-format m (qr-format-bits 0)
    in m

  qr5-encode : Text -> Integer
  qr5-encode (s) = qr5-encode-part "" s 0 (text-length s)

 The ECC tail of the codeword buffer, exposed for the battery test
 to pin against an independent Reed-Solomon computation.

  qr5-ecc-of : Text -> Integer
  qr5-ecc-of (s) =
    let gf = qr-gf-new
    in let gen = qr-rs-gen gf
    in qr-rs-encode gf gen (qr-make-data s)

Section: Text Dump

 One matrix row as a line of 0s and 1s, for serial verification and
 the battery's frozen expectation.

  qr-row-text : Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  qr-row-text (m) (r) (c) (acc) =
    if c >= qr-size then acc
    else qr-row-text m r (c + 1) (acc & (if peek-byte m (qr-at r c) == 0 then "0" else "1"))

Section: Rendering

 White quiet zone of four modules on every side -- the spec minimum,
 and the thing screens are usually worst at giving a camera. Dark
 modules are true black on true white for the full contrast the
 sensor can get.

  qr-draw : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-draw (base) (stride) (x) (y) (scale) (m) =
    let side = (qr-size + 8) * scale
    in let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride x y side side 16777215
    in qr-draw-mods base stride (x + 4 * scale) (y + 4 * scale) scale m 0

  qr-draw-mods : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  qr-draw-mods (base) (stride) (x) (y) (scale) (m) (i) =
    if i >= qr-size * qr-size then 0
    else let r = i / qr-size
    in let c = int-mod i qr-size
    in let w = if peek-byte m i == 1 then gop-fill-rect base stride (x + c * scale) (y + r * scale) scale scale 0 else 0
    in qr-draw-mods base stride x y scale m (i + 1)
